WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.473
Let A record the area accumulated under the rate two t

00:00:04.473 --> 00:00:08.916
Increasing the upper endpoint by a tiny amount adds a thin strip

00:00:08.916 --> 00:00:14.100
Its width is the input change, and its height is approximately the current rate.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.484
The thin-strip reasoning connects accumulation with differentiation

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:24.211
Here the area function is x squared, and its derivative returns two x

00:00:24.211 --> 00:00:31.700
At input two, the accumulated amount changes at rate four, matching the original rate at that endpoint.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.877
The other direction turns an integral into an endpoint subtraction

00:00:36.877 --> 00:00:43.468
Since x squared differentiates to two x, subtract its value at one from its value at three

00:00:43.468 --> 00:00:47.733
The change in accumulated area is nine minus one, or eight.

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:56.399
The pointwise derivative statement requires suitable regularity: continuity of the rate at the point is enough

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:01.923
At a jump, the accumulation can remain continuous but develop a corner

00:01:01.923 --> 00:01:06.067
The theorem's assumptions explain where this direct identification works.
